WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.490
- This is objectively a bad idea.

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.760
Delivering mail by having
teenagers jump off a moving boat,

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.070
run down a dock, put the
letters in the mailbox,

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:09.690
then leap back onto the moving boat

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.160
before it leaves them behind...

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.650
it's ridiculous. And not entirely safe?

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:15.750
And if someone suggested it
as a new thing, now,

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.600
in the 21st century,
it would never be allowed.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.240
But "mail jumping" is a summer tradition

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.430
here at Lake Geneva, Wisconsin, USA,

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:25.050
and it's also a tourist attraction.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.897
And today, I'm going to get to see
if I'd be any good at it.

00:00:27.897 --> 00:00:30.360
And it seemed like such a good idea

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.370
until I put the life jacket on.

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:34.980
- I have been mail jumping
for five summers now.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.890
This is my sixth summer at
the cruise line in total,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.170
but you have to work here for a season,

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.810
and then you can try out
to be a mail jumper.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.940
- Now, when this started back in 1916

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.310
there was about 60
houses on the entire lake

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.410
and that was the only
way they can get mail.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.470
We deliver to about
that same number, today.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:53.790
We're not postal employees.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.530
I'm an employee of the cruise line,

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.510
where we're contracted
to carry the lake mail

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.960
for that summer season,
which is June 15th to September 15th.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.410
So in 1916, the
predecessor of this company

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.180
got the mail boat, Walworth, and started

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.450
that daily, seasonal mail
delivery around the lake.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.040
As you can imagine,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.980
our share of five cents postage from 1916

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.200
wouldn't have financed
running a boat like this,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.930
and some forward-thinking
person came up with the idea

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.360
of making this a
passenger-carrying mail boat

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.170
'cause that's really what covers the cost
of making these deliveries.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.690
- The mail boat goes out
every day at 10am.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:30.240
Even on Sundays, we do
the Sunday papers.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.330
I do this about three or four days a week

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.570
and there are six mail
jumpers that do this job.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:38.460
If we get a big box to deliver,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.740
the captain will usually
slow down a little bit just

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.350
to make sure we don't
fall and hurt ourselves.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:44.820
But if the package is too big,

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.710
we will come to a complete stop.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.210
- Their shift starts at seven

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:51.900
so they start out by cleaning the boat,
cleaning the bathrooms,

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.120
getting all the supplies on board.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.233
Paige has to go through and
get the newspapers all laid out

00:01:57.233 --> 00:01:59.580
and put the pier numbers on.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.390
So all of these houses
have a street address,

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:07.500
but we correlate that street address
with their pier numbers.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.230
We had about 15 kids
try out for what ends up

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.060
being about five or six spots.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.100
They're a mail jumper and tour guide.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.020
We have them do some of the narration

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.730
so we can hear that they're articulate,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.170
then we have them do some jumps.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.450
They have to be somebody
that's pretty athletic,

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:24.200
but we let the tryouts
pretty much sort that out.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.780
- So I actually fell in when
I tried out for mail jumping,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.600
but they let me do it again
and get a second chance.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.700
So I got to redeem myself.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.320
You kind of just have to be confident.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.820
Don't hesitate when you're jumping,

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.400
because that's when you're going to
slip and fall and get hurt.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.360
- Before we set off,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.880
I did get some advice
from today's mail jumper.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.550
So leaping off, do I
need to match speed

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:45.900
with the dock or anything like that?

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.453
- It's not so much about speed.
- Okay.

00:02:48.453 --> 00:02:50.250
- It's just making sure that
you're not running at the boat,

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.580
because otherwise you're just going to
crash right into it.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:55.830
- So the jump off,
when I'm leaving the boat,

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.390
it's just, there's the dock.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.190
Try not to break your leg? Or...

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:00.941
- Yeah!
- Okay!

00:03:00.941 --> 00:03:02.820
- Don't try to jump and stop

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.740
because you're going to
be moving again obviously.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:08.220
So try to like run it out a
little bit, as we say, also.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.790
- And then it's just, so it's
jump, run, run, run, run, run.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:14.670
- Thing in the mailbox.
- It's going to be right there for you.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:15.600
Put it in.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.620
- Run, run, run and then?

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.670
- Turn.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.920
And then take a few steps,
you know, with the boat.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.160
- Okay. So try and match vaguely
some speed with the boat.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.680
And then it's leap and grab.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.720
- Yes.
- Okay.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.550
I'm going to say the words.
How hard can it be?

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:30.390
Let's do it.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.820
- So I have fallen in
the lake a few times.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:34.440
Not so recently, though.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:38.370
- On the average, probably once a week
or so, somebody will go in the lake.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.560
Most of the time when they miss,

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.780
they miss coming back onto the boat.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.330
So somebody's outgoing mail might get wet.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.790
We've got a little pool skimmer
down below.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.160
If we have to, we've had
somebody lean out the door

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.230
and scoop stuff back up out of the lake.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.900
The safety vests that they
wear are self-inflating.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.380
The moment they get
wet, bang, they blow up.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.277
Every pier that we deliver
to has a ladder on it.

00:04:02.277 --> 00:04:03.330
[chuckles]

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:07.920
And so when they miss, they
get themselves back on the pier

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.030
and I come back around.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:12.060
You know, you don't back up
to somebody that's in the water.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.560
They jump back on,

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.080
they wring out their socks
and we keep on going.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:18.570
They know that their mail
might get wet, someday.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.780
They know that their outgoing mail
might have tooth marks on it.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:23.370
- I think this is a great summer job.

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:24.203
It's really fun.

00:04:24.203 --> 00:04:25.560
I like being out here on the lake.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.390
Being out in the sun every day.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.510
- Right, my turn.
The tourists have left.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:33.150
The team here are just
giving me the same test

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.030
that they give to anyone
trying out for this job.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.800
It's just that the people
who try out

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.560
are usually half my age
and twice as athletic.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.020
So, you know, what could go wrong?

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.180
I'll promise this.
I'm only giving myself one attempt.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:46.740
If I nail this, you'll see it.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.410
If I miss the boat or
chicken out on the jump back,

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.360
you'll also see it.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.438
This is all I need to do.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.243
You made that look so easy.

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:07.972
[laughing]

00:05:07.972 --> 00:05:11.100
I like that you took the
time to kiss the goose,

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.020
like, that was style points.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:14.400
This isn't actual mail, right?

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.489
This is just-

00:05:15.489 --> 00:05:18.624
- Yeah. That's just a
extra newspaper for you.

00:05:18.624 --> 00:05:20.250
- Let's do it.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:22.200
I just realized that I'm
still wearing my microphone,

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:24.480
so I really do not want to miss this jump.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:25.980
Okay, so when do I start going out?

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:26.813
I can start.

00:05:26.813 --> 00:05:27.898
- Yeah, you can go ahead and climb out.

00:05:27.898 --> 00:05:28.770
- You can get on the ledge there.

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:29.820
There's a little stirrup here

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:32.043
that you can put your left foot into,

00:05:32.043 --> 00:05:33.779
'til you get ready to go all the way out.

00:05:33.779 --> 00:05:34.612
- All right.

00:05:34.612 --> 00:05:35.445
[laughing]

00:05:35.445 --> 00:05:36.600
The nerves just kicked in.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:37.433
- You got it.

00:05:38.727 --> 00:05:41.730
- And I want to lead with my
right leg out, left leg back.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.070
Okay. Oh, this is fast.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:46.663
This is so much faster
than I thought it was.

00:05:46.663 --> 00:05:47.896
[yells]

00:05:48.494 --> 00:05:49.411
Okay, whoa.

00:05:52.492 --> 00:05:54.354
- Oh, he did it.

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:55.187
- Yes!

00:05:55.187 --> 00:05:58.281
- Yay, that was a good one.

00:05:58.281 --> 00:05:59.597
- That was terrifying.

00:05:59.597 --> 00:06:01.250
[all laughing]

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:04.110
Of all the ridiculous stuff I've done.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.840
The bit that scared me
the most was the landing.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.210
Just not knowing, am I going
to just collapse forward?

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:10.623
Oh, there's the goose, okay.

00:06:11.658 --> 00:06:13.260
I don't want to do that again.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.690
How many times do you do that, Paige?

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:18.150
- 40, 50 times, yeah.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.810
- Thank you so much. Uh,
Lake Geneva mail boat tours.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:23.373
Link's in the description.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:25.417
My heart's going!

